        ":question/problem": "Simplify the trigonometric expression $\\frac{{sin}^{3}\u2061x+\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x{cos}^{2}\u2061x+1}{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x}.$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "First, we factor $\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x$ from the first two terms:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{sin}^{3}\u2061x+\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x{cos}^{2}\u2061x+1}{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x}=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x({sin}^{2}\u2061x+{cos}^{2}\u2061x)+1}{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the Pythagorean identity to rewrite the expression in the parentheses, and then we simplify the resulting expression as much as possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x({sin}^{2}\u2061x+{cos}^{2}\u2061x)+1}{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x+1}{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x}{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x}+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}\u2061x+\\operatorname{sec}\u2061x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{sin}^{3}\u2061x+\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x{cos}^{2}\u2061x+1}{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x}=\\operatorname{tan}\u2061x+\\operatorname{sec}\u2061x\n$$",
